+++ b/docs/decisions/0009-due-times.md
@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# 9. Due times on tickets
+
+Date: 2026-11-15
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The help desk is promising a first response by priority: 4 hours for urgent tickets, 24 for high, 72 for normal and 168
+for low. Agents need to see when each ticket is due and to list the ones that have slipped, and the leads need to change
+those hours without a release. Tickets already in the production data file must get a due time too. The new field must
+not strand operators: 1.4.0 has to start on a file this release wrote, and the new API must stay off until that file
+holds due times.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will store an absolute `due` time on each ticket, set once from its created time and the response hours for its
+priority.
+
+- The hours come from `DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL` and `DUE_HOURS_LOW` (defaults 4, 24, 72,
+  168). Changing the hours, or a ticket's priority, does not move a due time that is already set.
+- Tickets in the data file that have no `due` receive one on load from their created time and priority, and that write
+  is saved. The file version stays 1; `due` is an extra field 1.4.0 ignores.
+- Until `DUE_TIMES=on`, the HTTP API is unchanged: no `due` field, and `overdue` is ignored. The store still keeps due
+  times so the data file can hold them before the flag is switched on.
+- When the flag is on, every ticket includes `due`, and `GET /tickets?overdue=true` lists only open and in-progress
+  tickets whose due time has passed.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Operators deploy, wait until the data file holds `due`, then set `DUE_TIMES=on`. Unsetting the flag or going back to
+  1.4.0 still shows every ticket.
+- The command line's `show` prints `due`. `list --raw` is unchanged, so the weekly report keeps reading columns by
+  position.
